1. double backslash is a literal backslash: a\\b
2. a single backslash escapes the next char and is consumed: a\xb
3. escaping the first letter of a macro name prints it literally: \show
4. an unescaped macro call still works normally: show(value=Z)
5. an escaped comma stays literal inside a value: show(value=a\,b)
6. escaping required leaves it as literal value text, not an attr: show(\required)
7. an escaped left angle bracket cannot start a param list: show\<oops>
8. an escaped hash is just a literal hash: a\#b
